Amaro's JavaScript wrapper is MIT licensed (Marco Ippolito and Amaro
contributors). Its SWC TypeScript parser/transformer is distributed under
Apache-2.0. Both licenses supplied by the release are retained here.
The bundled index.js contains the actual SWC Wasm binary as base64 plus its
wasm-bindgen JavaScript imports; it is not a Node native addon. The upstream
source and Rust lockfile are pinned under the release commit's deps/swc tree:
https://github.com/nodejs/amaro/tree/aa10506eb6115cbc7571237d6d1cfff20753ef9c/deps/swc
Upstream build recipe:
https://github.com/nodejs/amaro/blob/aa10506eb6115cbc7571237d6d1cfff20753ef9c/tools/build-wasm.js
That recipe pins wasm-bindgen-cli 0.2.100 and the wasm-builder container digest.

Runtime integration: internal/typescript lazily requires this module, whose
WebAssembly.Module and WebAssembly.Instance execute on Silk's Wasm provider.
The only required JS modules are util (TextEncoder/TextDecoder) and node:buffer.
No parser source is evaluated as JavaScript and no Node/V8 subprocess is used.
The base64 asset is embedded with the internal JavaScript module, so deployment
does not depend on locating a separate .wasm file beside the executable.

The exact reference Node v24.22.2 binary reports process.versions.amaro=1.1.11.
Golden fixtures in tests/fixtures/typescript/reference.json were captured from
that binary's node:module.stripTypeScriptTypes, not from this vendored copy.
